@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.856 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1232 x i8] }> <{ [1232 x i8] c"Reads structured data from an XML file.\0A\0AThe XML file is parsed into an array of dictionaries and strings. XML nodes\0Acan be elements or strings. Elements are represented as dictionaries with\0Athe following keys:\0A\0A- `tag`: The name of the element as a string.\0A- `attrs`: A dictionary of the element's attributes as strings.\0A- `children`: An array of the element's child nodes.\0A\0AThe XML file in the example contains a root `news` tag with multiple\0A`article` tags. Each article has a `title`, `author`, and `content` tag. The\0A`content` tag contains one or more paragraphs, which are represented as `p`\0Atags.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#let find-child(elem, tag) = {\0A  elem.children\0A    .find(e => \22tag\22 in e and e.tag == tag)\0A}\0A\0A#let article(elem) = {\0A  let title = find-child(elem, \22title\22)\0A  let author = find-child(elem, \22author\22)\0A  let pars = find-child(elem, \22content\22)\0A\0A  heading(title.children.first())\0A  text(10pt, weight: \22medium\22)[\0A    Published by\0A    #author.children.first()\0A  ]\0A\0A  for p in pars.children {\0A    if (type(p) == \22dictionary\22) {\0A      parbreak()\0A      p.children.first()\0A    }\0A  }\0A}\0A\0A#let data = xml(\22example.xml\22)\0A#for elem in data.first().children {\0A  if (type(elem) == \22dictionary\22) {\0A    article(elem)\0A  }\0A}\0A```" }>, align 1
